That has a nice paradox to it. The last line changes the first.

Who are you?
I cried out into the void.
No one, it replied.

It can be read at least three ways:

* The void is empty, so the answer is literally “no one.”
* The speaker is hearing their own reflection, making the exchange about identity.
* “No one” echoes the archetype of the unnamed wanderer—someone defined less by a fixed identity than by their actions.

It also has a quiet circularity: if the void answers, perhaps it wasn’t empty after all. Or perhaps the act of asking created the answer.

It reminds me of the idea that some questions don’t reveal what’s “out there” so much as they reveal the model the asker constructs in response. The dialogue becomes part of the answer. That’s one reason such short pieces can linger—they leave just enough unresolved for the reader to finish them internally.